9. Fit Function

We need a way to determine whether reconstructed music actually belongs to the new film.

Define:

Fitness(M,F)=
w_1N+
w_2E+
w_3V+
w_4C+
w_5O

where:

* N = narrative alignment
* E = emotional alignment
* V = visual synchronization
* C = continuity across the film
* O = originality

A candidate that resembles the source strongly but fits the target poorly should lose.

A candidate that copies recognizable source expression should lose.

A candidate that sounds excellent independently but ignores the narrative should also lose.

The winner is the music that makes the target film work.

⸻

10. Temporal Mapping

This should operate continuously rather than scene-by-scene alone.

At every time t:

X_t =
[
V_t,
N_t,
E_t,
H_t
]

where H_t represents narrative history.

Generate:

M_t=P(X_t)

But music also changes what comes next:

M_t
\rightarrow
H_{t+1}

because once the audience associates a motif with something, future appearances carry that history.

Therefore:

X_t
\rightarrow
M_t
\rightarrow
AudienceMemory
\rightarrow
X_{t+1}
\rightarrow
M_{t+1}

The score is stateful.

